tion so the executor does not self-trip the gate. Add after line 217:\n```\n# (the pattern is a plain substring check: keep comments free of the literal strings `git push`, `git commit`, `git remote`)\n```\n\n## Strengths\n- **Fully container-autonomous.** Every `<verification>` line runs inside the mounted repo with no cluster/PR/Docker capability, and `make test` was confirmed to exit 0 under the repo's `hideGit: true` masking.\n- **Boundary-crossing contract test is the right shape.** The stub `git` is a real subprocess and the Success case asserts the exact argv (`clone https://github.com/bborbe/foo <expected dest>`) and the absence of `--recurse-submodules`, plus a filesystem-boundary assertion (`find <clone> -perm -u+w` prints nothing). This is level-2-style integration coverage of the `git` subprocess seam.\n- **The three \"verified while writing this prompt\" facts are all true** — each was re-verified (test-only package validity incl. golangci-lint v2, proxy env, GNU `find -printf`), which is rare and materially reduces execution risk.\n- **Anti-hallucination guard.** `<context>` explicitly states the upstream `agent-sentry-issue-analyzer/scripts/repo-clone.sh` is unreachable and must not be guessed at — this removes the most likely failure mode (inventing upstream code).\n- **Failure-mode traceability is complete** for every clone-relevant row of the spec's table, and the security/abuse section's defenses (charset, `.`/`..` rejection, symlink-resolve guard before any destructive call, non-writable tree) all map to concrete requirements.\n- **Cross-prompt coordination is explicit and conflict-free.** The `.gitignore` line this prompt adds is also required by AC11 (assigned to prompt 3); prompt 3 handles it idempotently (\"if the line is present, leave it alone\"), so the two prompts cannot produce a duplicate line. The shared `scripts_suite_test.go` content is byte-identical across prompts 1 and 2, and both are idempotent, so either run order works.\n- **YAGNI-clean.** No opt-out flags, no tunable knobs — the spec's Non-goals (\"Do NOT add a clone-directory, clone-depth, timeout or output-cap env knob\") are enforced in req 1, req 5, and the constraints.\n\n## Summary\nThis is an exemplary prompt: all DoD sections are present and high quality, every code reference resolves, the environment claims are independently verifiable (and true), and the prompt is fully executable by the read-only container agent with no operator-only or git-masked verification lines. The only findings are wording and test-strength improvements — chiefly the req 4 parenthetical about a non-existent `repos/` directory and the unasserted `head_sha`/`default_branch` round-trip. Priority action: apply Recommendations 1 and 2 before approve.\n\n### prompts/3-spec-001-config-allowlist-and-consistency.md\n\n# Prompt Audit Report: Align the Config allowlist, image and prose, and enforce the agreement with a test\n\n**File**: `prompts/3-spec-001-config-allowlist-and-consistency.md`\n**Score**: 8/10\n**Status**: Good\n\n**Audit-environment caveat:** the `dark-factory` CLI is not present in this audit container, so `dark-factory config` could not be run. Execution facts were derived from `/workspace/.dark-factory.log` (`hideGit=true hideGitSource=arg`, `workflow=direct`) and `/workspace/.dark-factory.yaml` (`workflow: direct`, no `hideGit`).\n\n## DoD Checklist\n- [x] `<summary>` present and plain-language (9 bullets, no paths/identifiers)\n- [x] `<objective>` states end state (what + why)\n- [x] `<context>` present (thorough, with exact anchors)\n- [x] `<requirements>` numbered and specific (1-9, exact paths, old→new snippets)\n- [x] `<constraints>` present (copied from spec)\n- [x] `<verification>` present with runnable command (`make test`, `make precommit`, plus AC greps)\n- [x] All paths repo-relative (no host-absolute, no `~/`); the `/home/node/.claude/...` doc paths in `<context>` resolve in the container and are the standard doc location used by the sibling prompts\n- [x] No bare `git ` commands in `<verification>` (repo runs `hideGit=true`; the file contains zero `git ` commands)\n- [x] No operator-only commands in `<verification>` (`kubectl` appears only in prose/objective and in a prohibition constraint — never in `<verification>`)\n- [x] File in `prompts/` inbox (not `prompts/in-progress/`)\n- [x] Filename not prefixed with a dark-factory global number; `3-spec-001-...` matches the allowed multi-prompt ordering pattern `^[1-9]-spec-\\d{3}-.*\\.md$`\n- [x] Filename reflects the primary change (`config-allowlist-and-consistency`)\n- [x] Status is `draft`\n\n## Failure Mode Coverage\n- [x] Spec failure modes addressed. The only spec failure-mode row in scope for prompt 3 is **\"`CLAUDE_ENV` missing a key in the applied Config (drift)\"**, addressed by requirement 1b (sets the value) + AC7 greps + requirement 7h (parse guard). The other rows (5xx, 401, crafted ref, re-clone, disk-full, huge response, hang) belong to prompts 1/2.\n- [x] Security concerns addressed: the spec's \"Trust boundary — `CLAUDE_ENV` gains only `GIT_REST_URL` and `PROMETHEUS_URL`\" is enforced by the exact-line AC7 grep (verification line 170).\n- [x] External calls have timeout/cancellation behavior specified — N/A (prompt 3 makes no external calls; its test parses files and calls a pure parser).\n- [x] Boundary-crossing contract test present for one of two boundaries: requirement 7h calls `envparse.KeyValuePairs` on the new `CLAUDE_ENV` value (good — this is the DoD level-1 contract test). The `ALLOWED_TOOLS` boundary is only shape-tested (see Recommendation 2).\n- [x] No `go mod vendor` in `<requirements>` or `<verification>`\n- [x] No cross-repo writes (no external clone + push; the only `git clone` is inside `repo-clone.sh`, exercised with a stub)\n- [x] Container-autonomous: no operator-only steps, no operator-vs-agent split. AC12 is correctly deferred to the operator with an explicit \"do not attempt it\" constraint.\n- [x] Sibling entry points covered: the prompt explicitly reads **both** entry points (`main.go` and `cmd/run-task/main.go`) and states no Go change; requirement 7h mirrors `main.go`'s call form. No gate/validation function is edited, so no parity gap.\n\n## Documentation Placement\n- [x] No inlined reusable coding patterns (>10 lines). Only YAML/grep snippets, no Go.\n- [x] Existing project docs referenced (`docs/observation-scripts.md`, `docs/dod.md`)\n- [x] Existing coding-plugin docs referenced (`changelog-guide.md`, `go-testing-guide.md`, `go-precommit.md`)\n- [!] Domain knowledge that outlives the prompt is mostly in `docs/observation-scripts.md` (good), but two repo docs keep the superseded `ClaudeRunnerConfig.Env` advice — see Recommendation 3.\n\n## Code Reference Verification\n\n| Reference | File | Status |\n|-----------|------|--------|\n| `docs/observation-scripts.md` \"How the scripts get their env\" | Verified | Correct — exists, section present (lines 9-16) |\n| `k8s/defect-diagnoser-agent-config.yaml` (ALLOWED_TOOLS line 41, env lines 46-49, path-3 comment lines 54-57) | Verified | Correct |\n| `agent/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"(task files, Prometheus, pod logs/events)\" | Verified | Correct — line 15 |\n| `README.md` \"Observes\" line 7, `ClaudeRunnerConfig.Env` line 68, `### Claude subprocess env allowlist` line 66 | Verified | Correct |\n| `CHANGELOG.md` `## v0.0.0` | Verified | Correct — line 3 |\n| `Dockerfile` `apk --no-cache add` line 12; final stage `FROM alpine` resolving to the named stage | Verified | Correct |\n| `.gitignore` (no `/agent/repos/` today) | Verified | Correct — line must be added |\n| `main.go` `envparse.KeyValuePairs(a.ClaudeEnvRaw)` | Verified | Correct — line 141 |\n| `cmd/run-task/main.go` `envparse.KeyValuePairs(a.ClaudeEnvRaw)` | Verified | Correct — line 85 |\n| `pkg/prompts/workflow.md`, `pkg/prompts/output-format.md` | Verified | Correct — exist, mention no `scripts/*.sh` |\n| `pkg/factory/factory_suite_test.go`, `pkg/factory/factory_test.go` | Verified | Correct — exist |\n| `pkg/scripts/scripts_suite_test.go` | Forward reference | Does **not** exist at audit time; deliverable of sibling prompt 1/2. Acceptable given prompt 3 depends on both, and the prompt states this explicitly. |\n| `docs/dod.md` | Verified | Correct |\n| `/home/node/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, coding-plugin `changelog-guide.md` / `go-testing-guide.md` / `go-precommit.md` | Verified | Correct — all resolve in the container |\n\n## Coding Guidelines Compliance\n\n| Guideline | File | Status |\n|-----------|------|--------|\n| External `_test` package + Ginkgo/Gomega suite | `go-testing-guide.md` | Compliant — requirement 7 mandates `package scripts_test`, reuse of the suite file, and `It` bodies |\n| Linter limits on test files (`funlen` 80/50, `gocognit` 20, `nestif` 4, golines 100) | `go-precommit.md` | Compliant — requirement 8 addresses all four |\n| No inlined `fmt.Errorf`, HTTP handler, factory, or error-wrapping code | `go-error-wrapping-guide.md` / `go-http-handler*` / `go-factory-pattern.md` | N/A — prompt changes no production Go |\n| No test-only package-level mutable state (`var X` + `SetX`) | `go-composition.md` / `go-time-injection.md` | N/A — none introduced |\n| No `go mod vendor` | `go-precommit.md` / global rule | Compliant |\n\n## YAGNI Pass (scope-creep detector)\n- [x] No opt-out flag for the behavior the prompt ships\n- [x] No config field / threshold / branch without a spec consumer. The `.gitignore` \"add only if absent\" conditional is cross-prompt coordination (prompt 2 also adds the line), not a knob.\n- [!] One item judged **in-scope, not scope creep**: requirement 7h (parse `CLAUDE_ENV` via `envparse.KeyValuePairs`). See the dedicated finding below.\n\n## Findings on the two flagged items\n\n### Flagged item 1 — AC8 exact-string grep: the creator's reasoning is WRONG (Recommendation)\n\nThe spec's AC8 line 95 escapes the asterisks:\n\n```\ngrep -c 'ALLOWED_TOOLS: \"…vault-read.sh:\\*),…vault-list.sh:\\*),…prometheus-query.sh:\\*),…repo-clone.sh:\\*)\"' k8s/defect-diagnoser-agent-config.yaml\n```\n\n`cat -A` on the raw spec confirms the bytes are `:\\*`, not `:*`. In BRE, `\\*` is a **literal** asterisk, so the spec's command compiles to exactly the intended string. It was tested empirically against a file containing the new allowlist line:\n\n- spec command (escaped `\\*`) → `1` (passes)\n- bare-`*` command (what the creator claims the spec says) → `0`\n- prompt's `grep -cF` command → `1` (passes)\n\nSo the premise \"the AC's stated `1` is unreachable with that exact command\" is false: the spec's command does return 1. The prompt's `-F` substitution is **functionally correct and harmless** (and arguably clearer), but it is not a fix for a broken AC, and the `<context>` comment (lines 56-60) states something untrue about the spec. Two smaller inaccuracies sit alongside it:\n\n- Line 179 comment claims plain grep reads `(`, `)` and `*` as BRE metacharacters — `(` and `)` are literal in BRE; only `*` is a quantifier.\n- Line 119 says a shell grep with `:*` \"would silently match nothing\" — true only for a bare `*`; `grep -F` (or the spec's `\\*`) matches fine. The design decision (parse-then-compare) is sound; the stated reason is imprecise.\n\n**Fix (Recommendation — correct the justification; the `-F` command may stay):**\n\nReplace lines 51-60:\n\n```markdown\n<!-- Two deliberate deviations from the spec's literal AC text, both preserving the AC's intent:\n\n     1. AC7/AC11 write the leading whitespace as `\\s`. `[[:space:]]` is used here instead:\n        portable across grep builds and asserts the identical thing.\n\n     2. AC8's exact-string check is written in the spec as `grep -c '<allowlist>'`. Plain\n        `grep` is BRE, where `*` is a quantifier — the pattern `Bash(scripts/vault-read.sh:*)`\n        compiles to \"…vault-read.sh\" + zero-or-more `:` + \")\", which does NOT match the file\n        and returns 0, so the AC's stated `1` is unreachable with that exact command.\n        `grep -cF` (fixed string) returns the intended 1 and is verified to do so. -->\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```markdown\n<!-- Two deviations from the spec's literal AC text, both preserving the AC's intent:\n\n     1. AC7/AC11 write the leading whitespace as `\\s`. `[[:space:]]` is used here instead:\n        portable across grep builds and asserts the identical thing.\n\n     2. AC8's exact-string check is kept as `grep -cF` (fixed string) for readability. The\n        allowlist contains `*`, which plain grep treats as a BRE quantifier; the spec already\n        escapes it (`:\\*`), so the spec's command also passes — `-F` simply removes the\n        escaping subtlety and is verified to return 1. -->\n```\n\n### Flagged item 2 — requirement 7h: in-scope, not scope creep (Recommendation)\n\nRequirement 7h asserts that the Config's `CLAUDE_ENV` value, parsed with `envparse.KeyValuePairs`, yields both `GIT_REST_URL` and `PROMETHEUS_URL`. Judgement: **in-scope and defensible**, for three reasons:\n\n1. It maps directly to the spec's Failure Modes row *\"`CLAUDE_ENV` missing a key in the applied Config (drift)\"*. The spec's stated detection is the AC7 grep; 7h strengthens detection without contradicting it.\n2. It is the DoD's level-1 **boundary-crossing contract test**: the new `CLAUDE_ENV` string is passed to a library parser (`envparse.KeyValuePairs`, `main.go:141`), and 7h calls that parser on the exact value. That is precisely what the DoD asks for, and the author is right that a grep-only check would let a value that \"greps correctly but parses into one key\" ship.\n3. It is container-executable and is *not* AC12: AC12 proves the value reaches the subprocess `cmd.Env` (needs the router token + runner), which 7h does not attempt. The prompt's framing (\"the container-side guard for the seam that the operator-side acceptance check proves on the host\") is accurate.\n\n**Minor caveat (Recommendation):** it is beyond AC7's literal text, and the constraint at line 152 says \"Do not attempt [AC12]\". Add one clause to requirement 7h so the reviewer sees this is intentional: e.g. append *\"This is a container-side parse guard, deliberately stronger than AC7's greps and distinct from AC12 (which needs the runner and a token).\"*\n\n## Critical Issues\n\nNone. The prompt is container-executable, its verification commands all pass deterministically, and no requirement is impossible or contradictory.\n\n## Recommendations\n\n1. **Correct the AC8 deviation comment** — exact find/replace given under \"Flagged item 1\" above. (The creator's stated reason is factually wrong: the spec escapes the asterisks, so the spec's command passes.) Optionally also tighten the inline comment at line 179 to say only `*` is a BRE metacharacter.\n2. **Add a contract test through the real `ALLOWED_TOOLS` boundary.** The prompt contract-tests the `envparse` boundary (7h) but only *shape*-tests the allowlist boundary: requirement 7b/7c parse the value with the author's own regex `^Bash\\(scripts/(.+):\\*\\)$`, while production parses the same string through `claudelib.ParseAllowedTools` (`main.go:158`, `cmd/run-task/main.go:102`). Add to requirement 7 a sub-check, e.g.:\n\n   > Assert that `claudelib.ParseAllowedTools(<extracted value>)` yields exactly the four `Bash(scripts/…)` tools. This is the level-1 contract test for the boundary the production path actually uses, rather than a re-assertion of the test's own regex.\n\n   Not raised to Critical because the four entries are frozen/pre-existing and the `Bash(scripts/<name>.sh:*)` form is documented as the accepted form in `docs/observation-scripts.md`, so no new value shape is introduced.\n3. **Update the two repo docs that still carry the superseded `ClaudeRunnerConfig.Env` advice.** The prompt removes that advice from `README.md` (and AC11 asserts it is gone from README only), but it survives in `NEXT-DIRECTIONS.md:19` (\"Env forwarding — `GIT_REST_URL`, `PROMETHEUS_URL` must be threaded via `ClaudeRunnerConfig.Env`\") and `docs/creating-claude-agents.md:100,229,246`. Per the repo DoD (\"grep the entire repo and update all references\"), add a step to requirement 5, e.g.:\n\n   > d. Update `NEXT-DIRECTIONS.md`'s v1 \"Env forwarding\" bullet (line 19) and `docs/creating-claude-agents.md` (lines 100, 229, 246) to name `CLAUDE_ENV` as the operator path, so they no longer contradict the shipped mechanism.\n\n   (`docs/creating-claude-agents.md` is a generic template-forking guide, so if the spec intends to leave it alone, note that decision explicitly instead.)\n4. **Clarify requirement 7h's scope** — one clause, per the caveat under \"Flagged item 2\".\n5. **Minor:** requirement 1c and 7g are consistent (the `pods/log` RBAC mention does not match `pod[- ]?logs`, so describing the ServiceAccount's future permissions is safe) — the prompt already warns about this correctly; no change needed, just confirming the negative check is satisfiable.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- Every required section present and well-formed; frontmatter uses the canonical `spec: [\"001-read-only-observation-toolkit\"]` form, `status: draft`, and only the three allowed inbox fields.\n- `<context>` is exemplary: exact file + line anchors, an explicit note that `pkg/scripts/scripts_suite_test.go` is shared and must not be duplicated, and documented deviations.\n- `<verification>` is fully container-executable (no git, no docker/kubectl/buca), and every AC check is a deterministic `test \"$(grep …)\" = …` assertion that distinguishes before/after.\n- Requirement 7h is a genuine boundary contract test for the `envparse.KeyValuePairs` parser — exactly the DoD's level-1 pattern, and correctly distinguished from the operator-side AC12.\n- Cross-prompt coordination is handled: `.gitignore`'s `/agent/repos/` is added idempotently (prompt 2 also adds it), and the shared suite file is protected against double `RunSpecs`.\n- Correctly excludes `README.md` from the `scripts/<name>.sh` prose scan (its example table names other agents' scripts) and correctly excludes `NEXT-DIRECTIONS.md` from the `pod[- ]?logs` negative scan (v2 legitimately names pod logs).\n\n## Summary\n\nThis is a strong, ship-ready prompt: complete DoD structure, verified code references, container-executable verification, and good failure-mode/security traceability. The one factual defect the creator flagged is the opposite of what they believed — the spec's AC8 command already escapes the `*` as `\\*` and passes as written; the prompt's `grep -cF` works but its justification comment (lines 56-60) is untrue and should be corrected. Priority actions: fix that comment, add a `claudelib.ParseAllowedTools` contract assertion, and update `NEXT-DIRECTIONS.md`/`docs/creating-claude-agents.md`, which still carry the superseded `ClaudeRunnerConfig.Env` advice.\n\n---\n\n**Spec status:** left at `generating` — `DARK_FACTORY_MANAGED=true` is set, so the host-side generator performs the `approved → prompted` transition after this container exits. Per the command's instructions I did not retry, rewrite, or merge any prompt based on audit findings; the human reviewer owns that decision.","ttft_ms":4110,"type":"result","duration_ms":128646,"uuid":"5296a6fa-2f20-4249-8df5-86705dd14277","ttft_stream_ms":972,"time_to_request_ms":19,"first_content_frame_ms":972,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":3}
